  // A first look at the ring: a bore too wide to see all of at once, with a second cylinder down the
  // middle of it, and the ship flying in the sleeve between the two. The outer wall is the floor and
  // the core hangs overhead as the ceiling, so the jump the flat sections taught is the way between
  // them - and the core hides the far side of the bore, so a wide tube stops being an easy one.
  // Like Loop the Loop, this shows off a style early rather than teaching a mechanic: everything in
  // it is a block, a target or a jump. The one new word is the collar, a block the whole way round a
  // wall, which is the ring's version of a flat section's full-width wall and the thing that makes
  // the jump compulsory rather than optional. Collars come in pairs, outer wall then core, so the
  // rhythm is up-and-down rather than a single dodge.
  // Every ring here leaves the same twelve units between its walls that a flat section leaves
  // between its floor and ceiling, so a jump and a collar mean here what they meant there. What
  // changes is the bore: it goes from twenty to thirty to forty, and the core grows with it to keep
  // the room the same, so the walls flatten under the ship and the core turns from a pipe overhead
  // into a wall of its own. In the biggest bore, power-ups come in fours round the ring, one for
  // wherever the player happens to be.
